<h2>The backdrop</h2>
<p>The petitioner, claiming the KARIM and KARIM\'S marks with use traced to 1913, sued over a Moradabad restaurant run as GULSHAN-E-KARIM. The suit was filed in August 2022 and the written statement that October. An interim injunction followed in January 2025, affirmed with a disclaimer arrangement by a Division Bench later that year. Then, after the plaintiff had closed its evidence, the defendant sought leave to file twenty-five documents dated 1998 to 2017 — his own licences, tax records, bank statements and invoices — saying he had found them during renovation work. The Commercial Court allowed the application on payment of ₹20,000 costs. The High Court reversed.</p>

<h2>The sequence the statute demands</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1 — Custody at the time of pleading.</strong> Were the documents in the party\'s power, possession, control or custody when the plaint or written statement was filed? A party\'s own business records at his own premises answer themselves.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2 — Reasonable cause.</strong> If they were in custody, leave requires a cause outside the party\'s control for not filing them — not forgetfulness, not disorder, not later realisation of their utility.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3 — Relevance is not the test.</strong> However probative the documents, relevance cannot replace the statutory inquiry; treating it as the test is a jurisdictional error.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the discipline matters</h2>
<p>The 2015 Act\'s declaration-and-disclosure regime — the statement of truth, the obligation to disclose documents helpful and harmful alike, the bar on relying on undisclosed documents without leave — is the backbone of the commercial trial timetable. If a shifting explanation and a costs order could reopen the record years later, every deadline in the schedule becomes advisory. The judgment also illustrates the narrow but genuine role of Article 227 after the Act: appeals and revisions against interlocutory orders are barred, yet a Commercial Court that substitutes its own test for the statutory one commits an error the High Court can and will correct — a principle drawn from the settled supervisory jurisprudence the court applied.</p>

<h2>Checklist for commercial litigants</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Front-load the record:</strong> audit every file, ledger, mailbox and storage site before pleadings are signed; the statement of truth covers the search, not merely the selection.</li>
  <li><strong>If documents surface late, explain precisely:</strong> one consistent, dated, verifiable account of where they were, why they were inaccessible, and when they emerged. Shifting narratives were fatal here.</li>
  <li><strong>Oppose with the statute, not adjectives:</strong> the winning objection is that the applicant has not pleaded custody and cause in the statutory sequence — not that the documents are inconvenient.</li>
  <li><strong>Calibrate the challenge:</strong> a wrongful grant of leave is worth an Article 227 petition only where the error is jurisdictional; routine disagreements will not clear the supervisory threshold.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> The decision leaves the merits — including the defendant\'s claimed adoption of the name since 1997 — entirely open. What it closes is the route by which a party sits on its own records and deploys them after the other side\'s evidence is in. In commercial litigation, the file you build before filing is, very often, the trial.</p>
</div>
